<mxCell id="rulesBox" value="6 KEY DESIGN RULES FOR EMAIL DELIVERY SYSTEM&#10;&#10;1. OUTBOX PATTERN FOR RELIABLE DELIVERY (MANDATORY AT-LEAST-ONCE): User sends email → INSERT INTO outbox table (durable PostgreSQL storage) BEFORE Kafka publish → Kafka.send(email.outbound) → SMTP workers consume → on success DELETE FROM outbox, on failure keep in outbox for retry. WHY: If Kafka publish succeeds but server crashes before DB commit → email lost forever. Outbox guarantees email survives crashes. Retry strategy: exponential backoff (1 min → 5 min → 15 min → max 10 attempts over 24 hours). Permanent failures (550 User not found) → send bounce notification to sender. DLQ after max retries for manual investigation. Idempotency via Message-ID header prevents duplicate delivery.&#10;&#10;2. SLA-BASED PRIORITY ROUTING (HIGH/NORMAL/LOW QUEUES): Outbox Delivery Orchestrator determines priority: HIGH priority target &lt;1 min delivery (same domain gmail→gmail internal routing, known contacts in sender address book, VIP senders boss emails) → 50 dedicated SMTP workers. NORMAL priority target 5 min (external first-time contact gmail→yahoo, no prior interaction, no urgent keywords) → 100 workers. LOW priority target 30 min (bulk sends 100+ recipients, newsletter with List-Unsubscribe header, marketing campaigns) → 20 workers rate limited. Auto-suggestion correlation: recipient in contacts → HIGH priority for better UX. Worker allocation: 50/100/20 for high/normal/low. Kafka topics: email.smtp.high, email.smtp.normal, email.smtp.low.&#10;&#10;3. SMTP AUTHENTICATION TRIPLE-CHECK (SPF/DKIM/DMARC PREVENTS SPOOFING): SPF Sender Policy Framework validates sending IP authorized for domain → DNS query dig TXT gmail.com returns v=spf1 ip4:74.125.0.0/16 ~all → receiver checks is sender IP in allowed range → PASS or FAIL. DKIM DomainKeys Identified Mail validates email not tampered → email includes DKIM-Signature header with domain signature → receiver fetches public key from DNS dig TXT default._domainkey.gmail.com → verifies signature matches email content → PASS prevents tampering. DMARC Domain-based Message Authentication enforces policy → dig TXT _dmarc.gmail.com returns v=DMARC1 p=reject rua=mailto:dmarc@gmail.com → checks SPF or DKIM alignment with From header domain → if both FAIL and policy=reject → 550 DMARC policy violation, if policy=quarantine → accept but mark spam, if policy=none → accept log only. Result: authenticated delivery blocks spoofing/phishing.&#10;&#10;4. KAFKA EVENT-DRIVEN DECOUPLING (ASYNC PROCESSING 200 OK IMMEDIATELY): User sends email → Mail Send Service returns 200 OK status SENDING immediately (user not blocked) → actual SMTP delivery happens async. Flow: Mail Send Svc → Kafka email.outbound → Outbox Delivery Orchestrator → Kafka email.smtp.priority → SMTP workers → Kafka email.inbound (receiver) → Mailbox Receiver DB → Kafka email.indexed → Elasticsearch → Kafka email.received → Notification push. WHY: Decouples services (Mail Send independent of SMTP worker availability), horizontal scaling (add more consumers per topic), at-least-once delivery guarantee (Kafka retains until consumed), prevents cascading failures (SMTP worker crash does not block user send action). Topics: email.outbound 100 partitions, email.smtp.* prioritized queues, email.inbound, email.indexed, email.received.&#10;&#10;5. DATABASE SHARDING BY user_id (HORIZONTAL SCALING BILLIONS OF EMAILS): Mailbox_items table sharded by user_id → user emails on same shard for fast folder listing (Inbox/Sent/Drafts), thread view (conversation grouping). Partition key: hash(user_id) mod N shards. Composite PK (user_id, email_id) ensures no duplicate emails. Indexes: (user_id, folder, received_at DESC) for folder listing, (user_id, thread_id) for conversations. Outbox table also sharded by user_id. Thread detection: check In-Reply-To and References headers for existing message_id → if match UPDATE thread append email_id, else CREATE new thread. Cache recent threads in Redis (message_id → thread_id 7 day TTL 95% hit rate) avoids DB query on every reply. Cross-shard queries only for global admin analytics, user search uses Elasticsearch not cross-shard SQL. Enables horizontal scaling petabytes of email data.&#10;&#10;6. S3 FOR ATTACHMENTS (NEVER STORE BLOBS IN DATABASE): Attachments up to 25MB stored in S3 blob storage NOT PostgreSQL. Compose Service: POST /attachments/upload multipart/form → upload to S3 s3://email-attachments/{user_id}/{email_id}/presentation.pdf → virus scan ClamAV → return {attachmentId, storageUrl}. Email table stores only url_attachment VARCHAR S3 URL pointer, not binary bytes. WHY: DB buffer pool contamination (attachment bytes evict hot index pages), backup disaster (25MB × 1B emails = 25 petabytes in DB backups impossible), inefficient (DB optimized for row lookups not blob streaming). S3: 11 nines durability, lifecycle policies (archive to Glacier after 1 year saves 90% cost), presigned URLs for direct upload/download bypass server. Keeps DB lightweight for metadata queries (subject, sender, folder, read status)." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#e3f2fd;strokeColor=#1976d2;strokeWidth=3;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=12" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="60" y="820" width="1780" height="260" as="geometry"/></mxCell>

<mxCell id="analogy" value="BEGINNER ANALOGY: Email System as Postal Mail Service&#10;&#10;Imagine a global postal service. COMPOSE SERVICE is the post office counter where you write your letter and attach documents (max 25 lbs package = 25MB attachment). OUTBOX is the sorting facility where letters wait in priority lanes: Express Lane (same city delivery &lt;1 day = HIGH priority same domain), Standard Mail (cross-country 5 days = NORMAL priority external), Bulk Mail (marketing flyers weeks = LOW priority newsletters). SMTP PROTOCOL is the standardized addressing format everyone worldwide agrees on (street, city, zip, country) so any letter reaches any mailbox. SPF/DKIM/DMARC are like sender verification stamps: SPF checks the return address matches the post office location (IP authorized), DKIM is the official postal seal (signature proves not tampered), DMARC is the policy (if seal broken reject or investigate). S3 BLOB STORAGE is the package warehouse (attachments too big for mailbox stored separately, you get claim ticket = S3 URL to retrieve). ELASTICSEARCH is the search index (librarian cataloged every letter by keyword so you find meeting tomorrow in seconds not hours). KAFKA QUEUES are the conveyor belts moving letters between sorting stations asynchronously (sender drops letter and leaves, does not wait for delivery confirmation days later). MAILBOX DB is your home mailbox organized by folder (Inbox, Sent, Junk Mail, Archive). Thread detection groups all reply letters to same conversation (original letter + 5 replies stapled together). Reliable delivery means if truck breaks down (server crash), letters waiting in sorting facility (Outbox DB) are not lost and retry next truck (exponential backoff) until delivered or return to sender after 10 attempts (bounce notification)." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#fff9c4;strokeColor=#f57f17;strokeWidth=2;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="60" y="1120" width="1780" height="160" as="geometry"/></mxCell>

<mxCell id="scaleNote" value="SCALE: 1.8B users, billions of emails daily, petabytes of storage in S3, millions of emails per minute peak, 99.99% uptime, 15GB quota per free user, 100 partitions Kafka email.outbound, 50/100/20 SMTP workers high/normal/low priority" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=#7b1fa2;fillColor=#f3e5f5;align=center;fontSize=11;rounded=1" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="320" y="420" width="720" height="50" as="geometry"/></mxCell>

</root></mxGraphModel></diagram></mxfile>
